---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import PdfToWord from '../islands/PdfToWord';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is the Word file editable?', a: 'Yes. The text is real Word text, not a picture. Headings use Word’s Heading 1–4 styles, so they show up in the navigation pane, and bullet lists are real Word lists.' },
  { q: 'Will it look exactly like my PDF?', a: 'No, and that’s on purpose. A PDF stores every letter at a fixed position, which turns into a mess of text boxes when it’s forced into Word. PDFSherpa rebuilds the document as normal flowing text, so it’s easy to edit, but columns, tables and images are not copied.' },
  { q: 'Does it work with scanned PDFs?', a: 'Yes. If the PDF has no text layer, PDFSherpa offers to read it with OCR first, in English, Urdu or Hindi. Check names and numbers afterwards, because OCR can misread blurry text.' },
  { q: 'Does it work with Urdu, Arabic or Hindi PDFs?', a: 'Yes. Text in any language is copied into Word, and Urdu and Arabic paragraphs are marked right-to-left.' },
  { q: 'Can I open the file in Google Docs or Pages?', a: 'Yes. The .docx file works in Microsoft Word, Google Docs, Apple Pages, LibreOffice and WPS Office.' },
  { q: 'Is my PDF uploaded?', a: 'No. PDFSherpa reads the PDF and builds the Word file inside your browser. Nothing is sent to a server, and the tool works offline after your first visit.' },
  { q: 'How do I turn the Word file back into a PDF?', a: 'Use <a href="/word-to-pdf">Word to PDF</a>, or choose File → Save as → PDF in Word.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="pdf-to-word"
  title="PDF to Word Converter – Free, Editable DOCX, No Upload | PDFSherpa"
  description="Convert PDF to an editable Word document (.docx) with headings, paragraphs and lists. Free, no signup, no watermark. Your file never leaves your device."
  h1="PDF to Word"
  lead="Turn a PDF into an editable Word document. Headings, paragraphs and lists come out as real Word text you can change, all converted on your device."
  howToTitle="How to Convert PDF to Word"
  steps={['Select or drop your PDF.', 'Click <strong>Convert to Word</strong>.', '<strong>Download</strong> the .docx and open it in Word or Google Docs.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['word-to-pdf', 'pdf-to-excel', 'pdf-to-text', 'ocr-pdf']}
>
  <PdfToWord client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What does PDF to Word do?</h2>
    <p>
      PDF to Word reads the text inside your PDF and rebuilds it as a normal Word document. Instead of hundreds of floating text boxes, you get
      paragraphs that re-flow when you type, headings that use Word’s own styles, and lists you can extend with the Enter key.
    </p>
    <p>
      It’s the right tool when you need to <strong>change the words</strong>: update a CV, reuse a report, fix a letter you only have as a PDF,
      or copy a long document into your own template. To change a few words and keep the exact look, use <a href="/edit-pdf">Edit PDF</a> instead.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Add your PDF.</strong> Tap <em>Select PDF</em> or drop the file on the box.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose page breaks.</strong> Leave <em>Start a new Word page</em> on to keep the original page boundaries, or turn it off for one continuous document.</li>
      <li><strong>Convert and download.</strong> The .docx is named after your PDF, for example <code>report.docx</code>.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>What gets converted</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Headings:</strong> text that is clearly larger than the body becomes Heading 1, 2, 3 or 4, by size.</li>
      <li><strong>Paragraphs:</strong> lines are joined back into paragraphs, and words split with a hyphen at the end of a line are put back together.</li>
      <li><strong>Bold and italic:</strong> emphasised words stay bold or italic, and centred lines such as titles and addresses stay centred.</li>
      <li><strong>Lists:</strong> bullet points become a Word bullet list. Numbered items keep their original numbers (1., 2., a), b)).</li>
      <li><strong>Page size:</strong> the Word document uses the PDF’s page size, so A4 stays A4 and Letter stays Letter.</li>
      <li><strong>Right-to-left text:</strong> Urdu and Arabic paragraphs are marked right-to-left in Word.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Images, tables, columns, colours and fonts are not copied. Page numbers printed on the pages are removed.
      For forms you only need to fill in, <a href="/fill-pdf-form">Fill PDF Form</a> is easier than converting.
    </p>

    <h2>Scanned PDFs</h2>
    <p>
      A scan or phone photo saved as PDF contains pictures of pages, not text. PDFSherpa notices this and offers to recognise the text with OCR
      (English, Urdu or Hindi) before building the Word file. Recognition runs on your device too.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>The PDF is read locally with Mozilla’s pdf.js and the Word file is created in your browser. Nothing is uploaded or stored.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
